{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\nDiscovered during RP-03 S5 T2 plan review (advisory, non-blocking, maintainability).\n\n`manifest_renderer.render_pass_outcomes` truncates the rendered `fuse_state` list to a bounded diagnostic size but does not emit a companion `fuse_state_truncated` count. A consumer reading a truncated manifest cannot tell entries were dropped, so the diagnostic silently under-reports open fuses.\n\n## Outcome\nEmit an explicit elided/truncated count (e.g. `fuse_state_truncated: <n>`) alongside the bounded `fuse_state` list so truncation is observable, mirroring other bounded manifest sections. Additive/versioned; existing field shapes unchanged.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] When `fuse_state` has more non-`None` entries than the diagnostic bound, the manifest carries an explicit `fuse_state_truncated` count of the non-`None` entries elided by that bound.\n- [ ] When not truncated, the manifest emits `fuse_state_truncated: 0`, matching the existing always-present `outcomes_truncated` convention.\n- [ ] A golden manifest test pins the new field for both truncated and untruncated cases."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788990834867301001,"uuid":"256aea58-34cb-45d5-a0ef-e28c4ad7fad5"}